## What this codeline is

This is the `gotransit` codeline: a Next.js application (`next`, `react`, `react-dom`, TypeScript)
serving GO Transit. The change under review touches the checkout surface at
`src/components/pages/CheckoutPage` — a React component tree using `react-hook-form` (with
`@hookform/error-message`), Stripe (`@stripe/react-stripe-js`, `@stripe/stripe-js`), and
internationalised strings via `next-i18next` / `react-i18next` / `i18next`. Content comes from
Contentstack (`contentstack`, `@contentstack/utils`), and shared pieces come from
`@metrolinx/cx-shared`.

Because this is Next.js, the execution-context question is real and specific here:

- A component under `src/components/pages/...` is a client-rendered React component, but Next.js
  also renders it on the server during SSR/SSG. Code that runs at module scope, or in a render
  path, executes in BOTH places. Anything that reaches for a browser-only global (`window`,
  `document`, `navigator`, `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, `Intl` locale side effects) outside a
  `useEffect` or an explicit client-only guard will throw during server render, not in the browser
  where the author tested it.
- `next` decides which environment variables reach the browser by name. A value read inside a
  component that is not exposed to the client bundle is simply `undefined` there — no error, just a
  branch that is wrong forever. Check the project's Next config and env files listed in the
  configuration surface above before assuming a value is available.
- Server-only packages in this dependency list — `@azure/search-documents`, `akamai-edgegrid`,
  `busboy`, `cloudinary`, `jsonwebtoken`, `micro`, `pino`, `next-auth` server pieces,
  `@contentstack/management` — must not be pulled into a component that ships to the browser, even
  transitively. Follow the imports of any newly imported module at least one level deep and ask
  what it does WHEN IT LOADS.
- Validation logic in `react-hook-form` (`validate` rules, resolvers, `mode` settings) runs in the
  browser on user interaction, but the same component module is evaluated during server render.
  Distinguish "the rule fires" from "the module loads".

Because this ticket concerns email / confirm-email comparison, pay particular attention to any
locale- or environment-sensitive string operation used in the comparison. `toLocaleLowerCase()`
without an explicit locale resolves differently depending on the runtime's locale, so a comparison
can behave one way under the server's Node runtime, another in a browser, and a third under Jest.
`toLowerCase()` has no such dependency. If the change relies on such a primitive, establish from
the code whether the behaviour is stable across the contexts the module actually loads in.

